      "stream_id": 4,
      "success": true,
      "response": "\n\n## Key Differences\n\n| Aspect | Async/Await | Threads |\n|--------|-------------|---------|\n| **Execution Model** | Cooperative, event-driven | Preemptive, OS-scheduled |\n| **Parallelism** | Concurrent (single-threaded by default) | Parallel (multiple CPU cores) |\n| **Overhead** | Low (no context-switching cost) | Higher (OS thread management) |\n| **Blocking** | Non-blocking I/O | Can block the thread |\n| **Memory** | Minimal per task | Significant per thread (~1MB+) |\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n### **Use Async/Await When:**\n- **I/O-bound operations** (network requests, file/database access)\n- **High concurrency** (thousands of connections)\n- **Web servers, APIs, real-time apps**\n- Example: Fetching data from multiple APIs simultaneously\n\n```python\n# Async - efficient for I/O\nasync def fetch_all():\n    await asyncio.gather(fetch(url1), fetch(url2), fetch(url3))\n```\n\n### **Use Threads When:**\n- **CPU-bound operations** (heavy computation)\n- **True parallelism** needed across cores\n- **Blocking libraries** that don't support async\n- Example: Image processing, encryption, numerical calculations\n\n```python\n# Threads - parallel for CPU work\nfrom threading import Thread\nThread(target=heavy_computation).start()\n```\n\n## Rule of Thumb\n- **I/O-bound → Async/Await** (better scalability, lower overhead)\n- **CPU-bound → Threads/Processes** (utilize multiple cores)\n\n**Hybrid approach:** Use async for I/O and threads/processes for CPU-bound tasks within async applications.",
